---
// /notes/: the NOTES.md template and what each section is for.
// Template text: dight-refactor/starter-kits/src/templates/NOTES.md (npm run sync).
import { getEntry } from 'astro:content';
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageMeta from '../../components/PageMeta.astro';
import CopyBlock from '../../components/CopyBlock.astro';

const template = await getEntry('templates', 'notes');
if (!template?.body) throw new Error('NOTES.md template is missing: run npm run sync');
---

<Base
  title="The NOTES.md template"
  description="Every assignment folder has a NOTES.md. Here's the template, and what each section is for."
>
  <PageMeta courses={['dight250', 'dight340']} />
  <h1>The NOTES.md template</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    The agent writes most of the code now. <code>NOTES.md</code> is where you show the part that’s yours: what you
    asked for, what you kept, what you changed, and what you still don’t understand.
  </p>

  <p>
    Every assignment folder (each Spark, each Prototype, and your Final Project) has its own <code>NOTES.md</code>. The
    template is already in your repo at <code>starter/NOTES.md</code>; copy it into each new folder. Spark 1 makes your
    first one, in <code>sparks/s01-repo-restructure/</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>The template</h2>
  <CopyBlock
    id="notes-template"
    text={template.body}
    buttonLabel="Copy template"
    copied="Copied. Paste it into a new NOTES.md."
  />
  <p>Replace <code>&lt;assignment name&gt;</code> and every placeholder line with your own words.</p>

  <h2>What each section is for</h2>

  <h3>Intent</h3>
  <p>
    What you built, who it’s for, and why, in 2–3 sentences. This is the first step of the loop. Write it <em>before</em> you ask the agent for code, and update it if your plan changes. On the Final Project, “did it solve the
    stated problem?” is graded against this.
  </p>

  <h3>AI use</h3>
  <p>
    Which tool you used, and your key prompts or instructions, copied as you sent them. Two or three that shaped the
    work are enough; you don’t need the whole conversation. This replaces the old <code>ai-notes.md</code>.
  </p>

  <h3>Accepted / changed / rejected</h3>
  <p>
    What you kept from the agent, what you changed, and why. This is the heart of the file. “I changed the <code>div</code> to a <code>button</code> because it couldn’t be reached with the keyboard” shows judgment;
    “everything looked fine” doesn’t. On a Prototype, a well-reasoned <em>rejected</em> decision is one way to earn
    Exceeds.
  </p>

  <h3>Dig deeper</h3>
  <p>
    One thing you looked up, with its MDN link, and what you learned from it. The agent ends most answers by pointing
    out something worth digging into, and <a href="/skills/dig-deeper/"><code>/dig-deeper</code></a> is built for
    this. Either way, you write it.
  </p>

  <h3>Open questions</h3>
  <p>
    What you still don’t fully understand. Honest gaps are welcome here and cost you nothing. I use them to choose
    questions for the 2-minute “explain this line” checks, so they help you prepare.
  </p>

  <details>
    <summary>See an example: a finished Spark 1 NOTES.md (DIGHT 250)</summary>
    <pre>{`# NOTES — S01 · Repo restructure

## Intent
I reorganized my course repo so this term's work has a clear home, and rebuilt my
hub page so anyone visiting can find my Sparks, Prototypes, and old projects.

## AI use
Copilot Chat in Agent mode. I pasted the Start prompt, then asked: "Why does
git mv keep the history when moving the folder by hand doesn't?"

## Accepted / changed / rejected
Accepted the folder layout as proposed. Changed the hub's heading from "Projects"
to "Archive" so it matches the folder name. Rejected its first link list, because
the cartoon link still pointed to /cartoon/ instead of /archive/cartoon/.

## Dig deeper
<main>: https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Element/main. There
should be only one per page, and screen readers can jump straight to it, which is
why the agent put my hub's link list inside it.

## Open questions
I'm still not sure why git mv shows a rename but moving the folder in Finder
looked like a delete plus a new file.`}</pre>
    <p>Short is fine. Each section says something only you could write.</p>
  </details>

  <div class="callout" role="note" aria-label="Your repo is public">
    <p>
      <strong>Your repo is public, and so is this file.</strong> Anyone can read your <code>NOTES.md</code>, including
      your Open questions. Write about the code. Leave out anything personal you wouldn’t post online.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Who writes it</h2>
  <p>
    You do, all five sections. Your <code>AGENTS.md</code> tells the agent never to write any of them. If you ask, it
    will ask you questions to help you write them instead. It can list the prompts you used, to help with AI use, but
    you write the section.
  </p>

  <h2>How it’s checked</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Sparks:</strong> Complete when the folder exists, its <code>NOTES.md</code> covers every section, and the
      live page loads.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Prototypes and the Final Project:</strong> a complete <code>NOTES.md</code> is part of “done,” and the Final Project
      rubric scores it under AI use.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Before you submit:</strong> run <a href="/skills/check/"><code>/check</code></a> on the folder. It flags
      missing sections and leftover template text, including placeholder lines, <code>TODO</code> markers, and HTML
      comments, but it won’t write anything for you.
    </li>
  </ul>
</Base>

